```elixir
geoframe_path =
  System.get_env("GEOSTATEX_GEOFRAME_PATH") ||
    Enum.find(
      [Path.expand(Path.join(__DIR__, "../../geoframe")), System.get_env("SPATEX_GEOFRAME_PATH")],
      &(&1 && File.dir?(&1))
    ) ||
    raise "set GEOSTATEX_GEOFRAME_PATH to a GeoFrame checkout before starting this notebook (or put geoframe next to this repository)"

Mix.install([
  {:geostatex, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:geoframe, path: geoframe_path},
  {:explorer, "~> 0.13"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## The question

The river Meuse floods the fields along its banks, and its water carries heavy metals from
upstream mining and industry. 155 topsoil samples taken in the floodplain near Stein, in the south
of the Netherlands, measure cadmium, copper, lead and zinc. They are the classic geostatistics
example (distributed with the R package sp; see `data/meuse/README.md` for its GPL-3.0 license).

Between the samples nothing is measured. Geostatistics asks:

1. How does the **difference** between two samples grow with the distance between them (the
   **variogram**), and is that the same in every **direction**?
2. What is the best **estimate** of zinc everywhere in the floodplain, and how **uncertain** is
   it (**kriging**)?
3. Does knowing the **distance to the river** improve the estimates (**universal kriging**,
   checked by **cross-validation**)?

Zinc is strongly skewed (a few very polluted samples), so, as is customary, we work with its
logarithm.

## 1. The samples

```elixir
require Explorer.DataFrame, as: DF
alias Explorer.Series, as: S
alias GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame, as: GDF
alias GeoFrame.GeoSeries, as: GS
alias Geostatex.{Kriging, Variogram}

df =
  [__DIR__, "data", "meuse", "meuse.csv"]
  |> Path.join()
  |> DF.from_csv!()
  |> DF.mutate(log_zinc: log(zinc), sqrt_dist: dist ** 0.5)

coords = fn column -> df[column] |> S.cast({:f, 64}) end
samples = GDF.new(df, GS.from_xy(coords.("x"), coords.("y"), crs: "EPSG:28992"))

DF.summarise(df, n: count(zinc), min_ppm: min(zinc), median_ppm: median(zinc), max_ppm: max(zinc))
```

```elixir
GeoFrame.Kino.deck(samples, color: {"zinc", :viridis}, radius: 5, tooltip: ["zinc", "dist"])
```

The most polluted samples lie along the river, on the west side of the map.

## 2. The variogram

The experimental variogram groups the sample pairs by distance and estimates, for each distance
lag, half the mean squared difference of their values. A model fitted to it gives the
semivariance at any distance: the **nugget** (differences at very short distance: measurement
error and micro-scale variation), the **sill** (the variance reached far away) and the **range**
(where it is reached).

```elixir
v = Geostatex.GeoFrame.variogram(samples, "log_zinc", n_lags: 15, maxlag: 0.6, model: :spherical, use_nugget: true)

%{range_m: Enum.at(v.cof, 0), sill: Enum.at(v.cof, 1), nugget: Enum.at(v.cof, 2), rmse: v.rmse}
```

```elixir
curve = for h <- 0..1600//20, do: %{lag_m: h, gamma: hd(Variogram.transform(v, [h]))}

points =
  for {b, e, n} <- Enum.zip([v.bins, v.experimental, v.bin_count]), e != :nan,
      do: %{lag_m: b, gamma: e, pairs: n}

VegaLite.new(width: 520, height: 300, title: "Variogram of log(zinc)")
|> VegaLite.layers([
  VegaLite.new()
  |> VegaLite.data_from_values(points)
  |> VegaLite.mark(:point, size: 60)
  |> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "lag_m", type: :quantitative, title: "lag (m)")
  |> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "gamma", type: :quantitative, title: "semivariance")
  |> VegaLite.encode_field(:tooltip, "pairs", type: :quantitative),
  VegaLite.new()
  |> VegaLite.data_from_values(curve)
  |> VegaLite.mark(:line)
  |> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "lag_m", type: :quantitative)
  |> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "gamma", type: :quantitative)
])
```

A spherical model with a range of about 900 m, a sill of 0.58 and no nugget: samples more than
900 m apart are as different as any two samples, and very close samples are almost alike.

Which model fits best? The same experimental variogram with other models:

```elixir
for model <- [:spherical, :exponential, :gaussian, :matern] do
  m = Geostatex.GeoFrame.variogram(samples, "log_zinc", n_lags: 15, maxlag: 0.6, model: model, use_nugget: true)
  %{model: Atom.to_string(model), range_m: Enum.at(m.cof, 0), rmse: m.rmse}
end
|> DF.new()
```

The spherical model fits best (lowest RMSE); the gaussian and Matérn fits need a small nugget and
the exponential one fits worst.

### Is the variation the same in every direction?

A **directional** variogram only uses the pairs whose separation points along an azimuth (here
within 22.5 degrees of it; azimuths are measured from east, clockwise, so 90 is north-south and 45
is northwest-southeast). If the range changes with the direction, the field is anisotropic.

```elixir
directional =
  for azimuth <- [0, 45, 90, 135] do
    d =
      Geostatex.GeoFrame.variogram(samples, "log_zinc",
        n_lags: 10,
        maxlag: 0.6,
        model: :spherical,
        use_nugget: true,
        direction: [azimuth: azimuth, tolerance: 45.0, model: :compass]
      )

    {azimuth, d}
  end

directional
|> Enum.map(fn {az, d} -> %{azimuth: az, range_m: Enum.at(d.cof, 0), sill: Enum.at(d.cof, 1), pairs: Enum.sum(d.bin_count)} end)
|> DF.new()
```

```elixir
dir_points =
  for {az, d} <- directional, {b, e} <- Enum.zip(d.bins, d.experimental), e != :nan,
      do: %{lag_m: b, gamma: e, azimuth: Integer.to_string(az)}

VegaLite.new(width: 520, height: 300, title: "Directional variograms (azimuth from east, clockwise)")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(dir_points)
|> VegaLite.mark(:line, point: true)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "lag_m", type: :quantitative, title: "lag (m)")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "gamma", type: :quantitative, title: "semivariance")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "azimuth", type: :nominal)
```

The range is longest north-south (about 1600 m) and shortest northwest-southeast (about 670 m):
zinc changes slowly along the river, which runs roughly north here, and fast across it, away from
the polluted banks. The kriging below stays isotropic for simplicity; `anisotropy_scaling` and
`anisotropy_angle` would take this into account.

## 3. Kriging

Ordinary kriging estimates the value at a point as a weighted mean of the samples, with the
weights that minimise the expected error under the variogram, and gives that error's variance.
Geostatex follows PyKrige here: its automatic variogram is fitted on PyKrige's own lags.

```elixir
ok = Geostatex.GeoFrame.kriging(samples, "log_zinc", :ordinary, variogram_model: :spherical, nlags: 12)
stats = Kriging.statistics(ok)

%{
  psill: Enum.at(ok.variogram_model_parameters, 0),
  range_m: Enum.at(ok.variogram_model_parameters, 1),
  nugget: Enum.at(ok.variogram_model_parameters, 2),
  q1: stats.q1,
  q2: stats.q2,
  cr: stats.cr
}
```

PyKrige's automatic variogram (on its own lags, with a different weighting) gives a shorter range,
about 580 m, with a partial sill of 0.48 and a tiny nugget. PyKrige's statistics krige each sample
from the ones before it: `Q1` (mean standardised error) should be near 0 and `Q2` (mean squared
standardised error) near 1 when the variogram describes the errors well. Here `Q2` is 1.10 and
`Q1` 0.34: the errors are about as large as the model expects, with some bias in the sequential
predictions.

Predictions on a 40 m grid, keeping the cells within 250 m of a sample:

```elixir
grid = Geostatex.GeoFrame.grid(ok, samples, cell: 40.0, max_distance: 250.0)

grid =
  grid
  |> GDF.put("zinc_ppm", grid |> GDF.pull("prediction") |> S.exp())
  |> GDF.put("std_dev", grid |> GDF.pull("variance") |> S.pow(0.5))

GDF.n_rows(grid)
```

```elixir
GeoFrame.Kino.deck(grid, color: {"zinc_ppm", :viridis}, radius: 3, tooltip: ["zinc_ppm", "std_dev"])
```

```elixir
GeoFrame.Kino.deck(grid, color: {"std_dev", :viridis}, radius: 3, tooltip: ["std_dev"])
```

The estimate (back-transformed with the exponential, from about 110 to 1840 ppm) is highest along
the river. The uncertainty (kriging standard deviation of log(zinc), up to 0.64) is lowest next to
the samples and grows away from them: it depends on where the samples are, not on their values.
(The exponential of the kriged logarithm estimates the median zinc, not its mean.)

## 4. Does the distance to the river help?

Leave-one-out cross-validation predicts every sample from the other 154, with the variogram of the
full model. Universal kriging adds the square root of the (normalised) distance to the river as a
drift term: the mean may change with it.

```elixir
sqrt_dist = S.to_list(df["sqrt_dist"])
opts = [variogram_model: :spherical, nlags: 12]

cv_ok = Geostatex.GeoFrame.cross_validate(samples, "log_zinc", :ordinary, opts)

cv_uk =
  Geostatex.GeoFrame.cross_validate(samples, "log_zinc", :universal,
    opts ++ [drift_terms: [:specified], specified_drift: [sqrt_dist]]
  )

DF.new(%{
  model: ["ordinary", "universal (sqrt distance to river)"],
  rmse: [cv_ok.rmse, cv_uk.rmse],
  mae: [cv_ok.mae, cv_uk.mae]
})
```

The drift lowers the leave-one-out RMSE from 0.417 to 0.400 and the MAE from 0.311 to 0.289:
knowing how far a field is from the river helps estimate its zinc.

```elixir
residuals =
  for {name, cv} <- [ordinary: cv_ok, universal: cv_uk], {r, i} <- Enum.with_index(cv.residuals),
      do: %{model: Atom.to_string(name), sample: i, residual: r}

VegaLite.new(width: 520, height: 220, title: "Leave-one-out residuals of log(zinc)")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(residuals)
|> VegaLite.mark(:boxplot)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "residual", type: :quantitative)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "model", type: :nominal)
```

## What we learned

* Log(zinc) is spatially correlated up to about 900 m (spherical variogram, almost no nugget), and
  more strongly along the river than across it.
* Ordinary kriging maps zinc over the floodplain with an uncertainty that grows away from the
  samples; PyKrige's statistics (`Q2` near 1) say the variogram describes the errors well.
* The distance to the river is a useful drift: universal kriging predicts the left-out samples
  better than ordinary kriging.

Every Geostatex function used here is verified against scikit-gstat or PyKrige (see the parity
guide).
